# text = But behold, it is better that thy soul should be lost than that thou shouldst be the means of bringing many souls down to destruction, by thy lying and by thy flattering words; therefore if thou shalt deny again, behold God shall smite thee, that thou shalt become dumb, that thou shalt never open thy mouth any more, that thou shalt not deceive this people any more.
# sent_id = 69
# llm_note = "behold" (token 2) = INTJ discourse on "better". "it is better" = expletive subject with adjective predicate: "it" expl, "is" cop, "better" root (adj). "that thy soul should be lost" = csubj of "better". "than that thou shouldst be the means" = advcl (comparative) of "better", "than" SCONJ mark. "of bringing" = obl of "means". "many souls down to destruction" = obj of "bringing", "down" advmod, "to destruction" obl. "by thy lying and by thy flattering words" = obl of "shouldst be" (means). Second main clause after semicolon: "God shall smite thee" = root of the consequent, linked as conj of the subject clause via "therefore if..." conditional: "if thou shalt deny again" = advcl, "therefore" advmod, "behold" (token 44) discourse on "smite". Three "that" purpose/result clauses after "smite": "thou shalt become dumb", "thou shalt never open thy mouth", "thou shalt not deceive this people" = advcl chains on "smite". Root of whole sentence = "better".
